  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nShowers and isolated thunderstorms in warm advection will\ncontinue to cross the area this morning. Elevated instability\nand a low level jet should also enhance some of this activity\nuntil just before sunrise. \n\nA digging upper trough is expected to advance out of the Midwest\ntoday, as a deepening surface low tracks across Southern Ontario\nto New England. The low will pull a strong cold front across the\narea from mid morning into the afternoon. Increasing flow with a\njet aloft will result in increasing shear, with building\ninstability east of the front. Enough heating/instability for\nstrong storms appear unlikely for much of Ohio, with an earlier\nFROPA. Further to the east, building instability and the shear\nshould result in a potential for storms to produce damaging wind\ngusts as the front crosses. Winds at 10kft are progged to be\naround 60kt at the time of FROPA across wrn PA, with around 50\nkt further to the south. \n\nThere is a potential for damaging winds as a line of showers and\nembedded thunderstorms develops along the front later this\nmorning into the afternoon. There is some veering in the lower\nlevels, with some limited potential for brief rotation should\nparameters briefly align, though the main threat should be from\ndamaging wind gusts. Gusty winds are also expected outside of \nany convection, with a tight pressure gradient and mixing. \nProbabilities of advisory level gusts have decreases according \nto the latest model data and BUFKIT momentum transfer \nprocedures. Still, gusts from 30 to 35 mph will be likely \nthorough tonight, with gusts around 40 mph across the higher \nterrain. The highest elevations of eastern Tucker county could \nsee gusts around 45 mph, but held off on a wind advisory at this\ntime with the recent downward trend in expected wind gusts. \n\nThe front should exit to the east by 4-5 PM, ending the severe\nstorm potential. The deepening upper trough is then progged to\ntrack across the Upper Ohio Valley region later today into \ntonight, with some lake enhancement also possible as cold\nadvection occurs. The precipitation should be tapering off by\nthe time the coldest air arrives Saturday morning, so other than\na potential for a few snowflakes mixing in north of I-80 and in\nthe ridges, no snow is expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper trough should exit the East Coast by late\nSaturday/Saturday night, as a low deepens/closes off the\nCarolina coast. Surface high pressure is expected to build\nacross the Upper Ohio Valley region, with light wind and mainly\nclear skies. This should result in efficient radiational\ncooling, with a frost/freeze potential across the area. Frost\nadvisories/freeze warnings may be needed. Some frost will be\npossible again Sunday night with surface high pressure overhead. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nModel ensembles indicate the closed low/trough should persist\noff of the SE Coast, with another trough over the Western CONUS.\nAreas from the Midwest to the Upper Ohio Valley region should be\nunder a ridge, with dry weather expected from Sunday into late\nnext week. Temperatures are also expected to moderate back to\nnear or just above seasonable levels by mid week.",
